course: space
lesson: 15
title: Pluto, the Kuiper belt, the Oort cloud and comets
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Beyond Neptune lies the Kuiper belt, a huge ring of icy bodies, and Pluto is one of the biggest of them, which is why it was called a dwarf planet in 2006: it shares its path with many similar objects.
explain: When New Horizons flew past in 2015, Pluto turned out to be a lively world with a heart-shaped plain of nitrogen ice, mountains of water ice and a thin blue haze.
explain: Much further out, scientists think a vast shell of icy bodies, the Oort cloud, surrounds the whole Solar System, perhaps a quarter of the way to the nearest star.
explain: Now and then one of these icy bodies falls in towards the Sun and becomes a comet: a dirty snowball a few kilometres across that warms up and grows a glowing head and tails, like breath steaming on a cold morning.
explain: Comets leave dust along their path, and when the Earth runs through that dust every year we see a meteor shower, such as the Perseids in August.
fact: Pluto was discovered in 1930 by Clyde Tombaugh, a young American astronomer, by comparing photographs of the sky taken days apart.
fact: In 2006 astronomers reclassified Pluto as a dwarf planet because it has not cleared its orbit of other similar objects.
fact: NASA's New Horizons flew past Pluto in 2015 and showed a heart-shaped plain of nitrogen ice, mountains of water ice and a thin blue haze.
fact: Pluto's biggest moon Charon is about half Pluto's width, so the two orbit a point between them, almost like a double planet.
fact: The Kuiper belt is a huge ring of icy bodies beyond Neptune, from about 30 to 50 times the Earth's distance from the Sun.
fact: In 2019 New Horizons flew past Arrokoth, a Kuiper belt object shaped like a snowman, the most distant object ever explored up close.
fact: The Oort cloud is a vast shell of icy bodies thought to surround the Solar System, reaching perhaps a quarter of the way to the nearest star; it has never been seen directly, but scientists think it is there because some comets keep falling in from that far away.
fact: Comets are dirty snowballs of ice, dust and rock a few kilometres across, which grow a glowing head and tails, often millions of kilometres long, when they come near the Sun and its heat turns their ice into gas and frees dust.
fact: A comet can have two tails, a curved dust tail and a straight blue gas tail, and both point away from the Sun.
fact: Halley's Comet returns about every 76 years; it last came in 1986 and will next be bright in 2061.
fact: Europe's Rosetta spacecraft orbited Comet 67P from 2014 to 2016 and dropped the lander Philae onto it, the first landing on a comet.
fact: Meteor showers happen when the Earth passes through trails of dust left by comets, such as the Perseids every August.
exercise: Who discovered Pluto, and when? | Clyde Tombaugh, in 1930 | William Herschel, in 1781 | Galileo, in 1610 | Jocelyn Bell Burnell, in 1967
exercise: How did Clyde Tombaugh find Pluto? | By comparing photographs of the sky taken days apart | By flying a probe past Neptune | By listening with a radio dish | By predicting where it was with maths alone, never looking at the sky
exercise: Why was Pluto called a dwarf planet in 2006? | It has not cleared its orbit of other similar objects | It stopped going round the Sun | It was found to be a moon of Neptune | It shrank to half its old size
exercise: What did New Horizons show on Pluto in 2015? | A heart-shaped plain of nitrogen ice | A huge ocean of liquid water on top | Erupting volcanoes of lava | A thick orange atmosphere like Titan's
exercise: Why are Pluto and Charon almost like a double planet? | Charon is about half Pluto's width, so they orbit a point between them | Charon is bigger than Pluto | They are joined together by a bridge of ice | They both orbit Neptune
exercise: What is the Kuiper belt? | A huge ring of icy bodies beyond Neptune | A belt of rocky asteroids between Mars and Jupiter | The ring round Saturn | A cloud of gas round the Sun
exercise: How far from the Sun is the Kuiper belt? | About 30 to 50 times the Earth's distance | About as far as the Moon is from the Earth | Just past Mars | Halfway to the nearest star
exercise: What is Arrokoth? | A snowman-shaped Kuiper belt object visited in 2019 | A moon of Jupiter that is covered in erupting volcanoes | The comet Rosetta landed on | A star in the Oort cloud
exercise: What is the Oort cloud? | A vast shell of icy bodies thought to surround the Solar System | A rain cloud above the Earth's poles | A gas cloud where new stars are born | The haze round Pluto
exercise: Has anyone ever seen the Oort cloud directly? | No, it is thought to exist but has not been seen | Yes, it is easy to see in the evening | Yes, New Horizons flew right through it and photographed it in 2015 | Yes, it shows up as a white band in the sky
exercise: What is a comet made of? | Ice, dust and rock, like a dirty snowball | Burning gas, like a small star that has wandered off | Solid iron and nickel, like a giant cannonball | Glowing water vapour and nothing else at all
exercise: Which way do a comet's tails point? | Away from the Sun | Always behind the comet's path | Towards the Sun | Towards the Earth
exercise: What are a comet's two tails? | A curved dust tail and a straight blue gas tail | A tail of fire and a tail of smoke | A tail of rock and a tail of water | A tail of ice and a tail of lava
exercise: How often does Halley's Comet return? | About every 76 years | Every year | About every 1,000 years | Once only, never again
exercise: When will Halley's Comet next be bright? | In 2061 | In 2030 | In 1986 | In 2100
exercise: What did Rosetta's lander Philae do? | Made the first landing on a comet | Made the first landing on Pluto's icy heart | Flew past Arrokoth and photographed it up close | Landed on Titan beside a methane lake
exercise: What causes meteor showers like the Perseids? | The Earth passing through dust left by a comet | Stars falling out of the sky and burning up in the air | Comets crashing into the Earth | Pieces of the Moon breaking off
test: A comet is racing away from the Sun. Which way does its tail point? | Ahead of it, still away from the Sun | Behind it, back towards the Sun it left | Down towards the Earth | Straight up, whatever the direction
test: my grandad saw Halley's Comet in 1986. if I'm 10 now (2026), how old will I be next time it's bright? | 45 | 76 | 10 | 86
test: In a cartoon a comet is drawn as a ball of fire burning through space. What is wrong? | A comet is icy and its head glows only when the Sun warms it | Nothing, comets are made of fire | Comets are made of burning coal | Comets are tiny stars that explode
test: Ella says Pluto got kicked out because it's too small to have a moon. what's wrong? | It has a big moon; it was renamed because it shares its orbit with similar objects | Pluto has no moons at all | Pluto was too big, not too small | Pluto stopped orbiting the Sun in 2006
test: Why did it take until 2019 to see Arrokoth up close? | It is so far that New Horizons needed years to get there | Nobody wanted to look at it | It only formed in 2018 | It was hidden behind Saturn until telescopes got bigger in 2018
test: If the Earth did not cross a comet's dust trail in August, what would happen to the Perseids? | We would not see that shower | They would be brighter than ever | They would happen in winter instead | The comet would fall onto the Earth
test: Which is the most distant object ever explored up close? | Arrokoth | Pluto | Comet 67P | Neptune
test: Pluto's heart is made of what? | Nitrogen ice | Red sand | Liquid water | Frozen lava
test: honestly why do they reckon the Oort cloud's real if nobody's seen it | Comets keep falling in from that far away | Someone photographed it with a big telescope in 1930 | It glows in the night sky | It is just a story with no reason
test: Which is true? | Comets are a few kilometres across but their tails can be huge | Comets are bigger than the Earth | Comets have no tails until they hit the Earth | Comets come from inside the Sun
test: Why does a comet grow a glowing head only near the Sun? | The Sun's heat turns its ice into gas and frees dust | The Sun's heat lights a fire on its surface, like a match | It only exists near the Sun | The Sun paints it with light
test: lol Rosetta landed on a comet? no way. what did it really do | Orbited Comet 67P and dropped the Philae lander on it | Flew past Pluto taking photos of its heart-shaped plain | Scooped up a comet and brought it back to the Earth whole | Crashed into Halley's Comet on purpose to see inside it
exam: Identify the principal reason for which Pluto was reclassified as a dwarf planet in the year two thousand and six. | It has not cleared its orbit of comparable bodies | It ceased to orbit the Sun | It was found to be a satellite of Neptune | Its diameter was found to have diminished
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the orientation of cometary tails. | Both tails are directed away from the Sun | Both tails are directed towards the Sun | They are invariably directed towards the Earth | They trail behind the comet in all positions
exam: State the interval at which Halley's Comet returns to the vicinity of the Sun. | Approximately seventy-six years | Approximately one year | Approximately one thousand years | Approximately seven days
exam: Ascertain the origin of the meteor shower designated the Perseids. | The passage of the Earth through dust shed by a comet | The gradual fragmentation of the Moon under terrestrial tides | The collapse of distant stars within the constellation Perseus | The eruption of terrestrial volcanoes during the month of August
